{"id": "2026-10-15-news-ukraine-lifts-pause-on-refinery-strikes", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Ukraine lifts pause on refinery strikes", "summary": "Zelensky told the FT Ukraine is ending its pause on strikes against Russian refineries after Trump's unconfirmed energy truce; Russia reported a fire at the Ryazan refinery and launched 580 drones at Ukraine.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/en-direct-guerre-contre-l-ukraine-elon-musk-estime-que-tout-ralentissement-du-reseau-militaire-ukrainien-est-du-au-gouvernement-americain", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["ongoing"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "President Volodymyr Zelensky told the Financial Times that Ukraine is lifting its pause on strikes against Russian oil refineries, and Russia reported a drone-and-missile attack on the Ryazan refinery overnight into Thursday 15 October. \"The ban is lifted. It's over,\" Zelensky said, as quoted by AFP. Ukraine had paused the strikes for several days after US President Donald Trump announced an \"energy truce\" on 11 October that neither Moscow nor Kyiv confirmed negotiating. Zelensky said he would stop again only if the US supplies Patriot interceptor missiles. Russia's Defence Ministry said falling drone debris set the Ryazan refinery, south-east of Moscow, on fire. Overnight, Russia launched 580 drones and two Kh-59 missiles at Ukraine, and Ukraine's Air Force says it downed or jammed 536 of the drones, with hits at 31 locations. The Kirovohrad region's governor called it the largest attack on his region since the full-scale invasion, with three people killed in Kropyvnytskyi. Ukraine's Navy said it hit the Borisoglebsk airfield in Russia's Voronezh region with Neptune cruise missiles and destroyed six Su-34M bombers, the jets Russia uses to drop glide bombs on front-line towns; it says that claim is still being verified. Trump has repeatedly criticised the refinery strikes, saying they push up world fuel prices.", "why": "Kyiv is openly breaking with Washington's line six days after Trump's 9 October deal to let Russian diesel reach world markets, which Zelensky said cost Ukraine \"a strong partner\".", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-ukraine-lifts-pause-on-refinery-strikes", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-10-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ukraine", "Russia", "Volodymyr Zelensky", "Donald Trump", "Ryazan"]}
{"id": "2026-10-15-news-peshawar-school-roof-collapse-kills-29", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Peshawar school roof collapse kills 29", "summary": "A school roof collapsed in Peshawar, Pakistan, killing at least 28 students and a teacher and injuring more than 50.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/15/school-roof-collapses-in-northwestern-pakistan-killing-at-least-29-people", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The roof of a private school in Peshawar, in north-western Pakistan, collapsed on Thursday 15 October, killing at least 28 students and a teacher and injuring more than 50. The building was the Al-Khair Education Centre, which had about 350 registered pupils. A spokesman for Lady Reading Hospital said all the children killed or injured were 13 or younger, and some of the injured are in critical condition. Rescue officials said everyone trapped under the rubble had been recovered. The authorities have ordered an investigation into the cause. Deputy Prime Minister Ishaq Dar said the government had \"taken serious note\" and that children's safety must remain paramount.", "why": "officials say the toll may still rise, and the investigation will decide whether the private school's building was ever properly inspected.", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-peshawar-school-roof-collapse-kills-29", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-10-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Pakistan", "Peshawar"]}
{"id": "2026-10-15-news-north-korea-fires-submarine-launched-cruise-miss", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "North Korea fires submarine-launched cruise missiles", "summary": "North Korea test-fired two types of submarine-launched strategic cruise missiles off its east coast, KCNA said.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/15/north-korea-tests-two-submarine-launched-missiles-state-media-reports", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["ongoing"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "North Korea test-fired two types of submarine-launched strategic cruise missiles off its east coast on Wednesday 14 October, state news agency KCNA said. One was a Pulhwasal-3-31, the same type of cruise missile Pyongyang said it fired from land last week. KCNA said the test checked the reliability of its \"war deterrents\" and had no effect on neighbours' security. North Korea is not believed to have a modern missile submarine fully in service yet; state media last year showed an 8,700-tonne nuclear-powered submarine under construction.", "why": "it is the latest in a run of tests, after ballistic missiles on 12 October and a hypersonic missile on 3 October, as tension with South Korea rises.", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-north-korea-fires-submarine-launched-cruise-miss", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-10-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["North Korea", "KCNA", "Pulhwasal-3-31"]}
{"id": "2026-10-15-news-seven-missing-after-black-sea-tanker-fire", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Seven missing after Black Sea tanker fire", "summary": "Romania said seven people are missing after a fire on the chemical tanker Wine Trader off Sulina, amid drone incidents blamed on Russia.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/en-direct-guerre-contre-l-ukraine-elon-musk-estime-que-tout-ralentissement-du-reseau-militaire-ukrainien-est-du-au-gouvernement-americain#wine-trader", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "President Volodymyr Zelensky told the Financial Times that Ukraine is lifting its pause on strikes against Russian oil refineries, and Russia reported a drone-and-missile attack on the Ryazan refinery overnight into Thursday 15 October. \"The ban is lifted. It's over,\" Zelensky said, as quoted by AFP. Ukraine had paused the strikes for several days after US President Donald Trump announced an \"energy truce\" on 11 October that neither Moscow nor Kyiv confirmed negotiating. Zelensky said he would stop again only if the US supplies Patriot interceptor missiles. Russia's Defence Ministry said falling drone debris set the Ryazan refinery, south-east of Moscow, on fire. Overnight, Russia launched 580 drones and two Kh-59 missiles at Ukraine, and Ukraine's Air Force says it downed or jammed 536 of the drones, with hits at 31 locations. The Kirovohrad region's governor called it the largest attack on his region since the full-scale invasion, with three people killed in Kropyvnytskyi. Ukraine's Navy said it hit the Borisoglebsk airfield in Russia's Voronezh region with Neptune cruise missiles and destroyed six Su-34M bombers, the jets Russia uses to drop glide bombs on front-line towns; it says that claim is still being verified. Trump has repeatedly criticised the refinery strikes, saying they push up world fuel prices.", "why": "Kyiv is openly breaking with Washington's line six days after Trump's 9 October deal to let Russian diesel reach world markets, which Zelensky said cost Ukraine \"a strong partner\".", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-seven-missing-after-black-sea-tanker-fire", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-10-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Romania", "Black Sea", "Wine Trader"]}
{"id": "2026-10-15-news-supreme-court-backs-credit-suisse-bonus-cuts", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Supreme Court backs Credit Suisse bonus cuts", "summary": "The Federal Supreme Court ruled the Finance Department could cut or cancel outstanding bonuses of about 1,000 former Credit Suisse managers, overturning the Federal Administrative Court.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/nach-cs-untergang-bundesgericht-doch-keine-boni-fuer-cs-kader", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Supreme Court ruled, in a judgment published on Thursday 15 October, that the Federal Department of Finance was allowed to cut or cancel the outstanding bonuses of former Credit Suisse managers, overturning the Federal Administrative Court. In May 2023, after the state-backed takeover of Credit Suisse by UBS, the department ordered the bonuses cancelled for the executive board and cut by 50% and 25% for the two levels below. About 1,000 people were affected. Several managers challenged the order, and in 2025 the Federal Administrative Court agreed with them, saying contractually promised bonuses needed a clearer legal basis. The Supreme Court disagreed. It said Article 10a of the Banking Act explicitly lets the government act on pay when a systemically important bank gets state support, and that this covers cancelling unpaid bonuses. The government had given Credit Suisse a default guarantee of up to 100 billion francs. The court said public money must not fund bonuses for the period when the crisis built up, and that the later repayment of liquidity aid does not change that. It also said the cuts are preventive, not a punishment, so no manager's personal responsibility had to be proven.", "why": "it confirms the state's power to block bonuses at any bank it rescues, while the Federal Council's proposed tighter rules on bank pay and manager accountability are still in consultation.", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-supreme-court-backs-credit-suisse-bonus-cuts", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-10-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Supreme Court", "Credit Suisse", "UBS", "Federal Department of Finance"]}
{"id": "2026-10-15-news-amaudruz-runs-for-parmelin-s-seat", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Amaudruz runs for Parmelin's seat", "summary": "Geneva SVP national councillor Celine Amaudruz announced her candidacy for the Federal Council seat Guy Parmelin vacates at year-end; the Federal Assembly votes on 9 December.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/ma-decision-est-murement-reflechie-celine-amaudruz-se-lance-dans-la-course-au-conseil-federal", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Geneva national councillor Céline Amaudruz announced on Thursday 15 October that she is running for the Federal Council seat Guy Parmelin will vacate at the end of the year. Amaudruz, 47, is a lawyer by training and a wealth manager, has sat in the National Council since 2011, and is a vice-president of the SVP/UDC (Swiss People's Party). She is the first declared candidate. SRF notes that other parties see her as electable, partly for her more liberal positions on social issues, but she votes with her party about 96% of the time. Zurich cantonal minister Natalie Rickli may still enter the race. The SVP parliamentary group picks its ticket on Friday 20 November, and the Federal Assembly elects Parmelin's successor on Wednesday 9 December.", "why": "her candidacy keeps the seat in contention for French-speaking Switzerland, and she would be the first SVP woman elected to the Federal Council.", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-amaudruz-runs-for-parmelin-s-seat", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-12-09", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Céline Amaudruz", "Guy Parmelin", "SVP", "Federal Council"]}
{"id": "2026-10-15-news-bern-adds-child-protection-to-platform-law", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Bern adds child protection to platform law", "summary": "The Federal Council will proceed with its law on very large communication platforms and search engines and add duties to protect minors, with a bill due by June 2027.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/NMn5s24l8NjQ", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "tech"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Council decided on Wednesday 14 October to go ahead with its law regulating very large online platforms and search engines, and to add duties to protect minors. Most parties, cantons and national associations backed the draft in consultation. The law covers only the biggest platforms and search engines, including AI search engines. Users must get an easy way to report any content they think is illegal. The new part requires these services to assess how far they create or worsen risks for minors and to reduce those risks. It also bans advertising to minors based on profiling, meaning ads targeted from their online behaviour and personal traits. The environment and communications department (DETEC) must present the bill to Parliament by the end of June 2027.", "why": "it would give Swiss users enforceable rights against platforms such as Google, Facebook and YouTube for the first time, and add duties to protect children, but the law will not reach Parliament before mid-2027.", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-bern-adds-child-protection-to-platform-law", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-10-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "DETEC"]}
{"id": "2026-10-15-news-climate-change-to-cost-ski-tourism-billions", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Climate change to cost ski tourism billions", "summary": "A FOEN-commissioned study estimates climate change will cost Swiss winter tourism over 2 billion francs a year by 2060.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/klimakosten-bis-2060-der-klimawandel-kostet-milliarden-fuer-wintersport-wirds-teuer", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "A study commissioned by the Federal Office for the Environment (FOEN/BAFU) estimates climate change will cost Swiss winter tourism more than two billion francs a year by 2060, SRF reported on Thursday 15 October. The study looked at three emissions scenarios and five risks: heat, drought, the rising snow line, heavy rain, and storms and hail. It assumes ski areas below about 1,500–1,800 metres will close sooner or later even in the best scenario. Farming losses could stay small if enough is invested in irrigation, and hydropower may produce slightly more electricity overall. FOEN climate-policy head Raphael Bucher said the total cost is likely \"significantly higher\", because the study leaves out some risks and does not model how risks reinforce each other.", "why": "it shows where spending on adaptation pays off, and where it does not, such as low-lying ski resorts.", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-climate-change-to-cost-ski-tourism-billions", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-10-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["FOEN"]}
{"id": "2026-10-15-news-swiss-share-data-on-4-million-accounts", "date": "2026-10-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss share data on 4 million accounts", "summary": "The Federal Tax Administration exchanged financial-account data with 113 states in 2026 under AEOI, adding Georgia, Moldova and Ukraine, with none for Russia.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/kJL3FzqwXiXV", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Federal Tax Administration said on Thursday 15 October that it exchanged data on about 4 million financial accounts with 113 countries in 2026 under the automatic exchange of information (AEOI), the global standard for sharing bank-account data between tax authorities. Georgia, Moldova and Ukraine were added this year. The exchange was two-way with 86 countries. The other 27 sent data to Switzerland but received none, either because they do not yet meet data-security standards (14) or chose not to receive it (13). Again, no data was exchanged with Russia. About 9,000 Swiss financial institutions report to the administration, and it received data on about 4 million accounts in return. It does not disclose the amounts of money involved.", "why": "cantonal tax offices use the data to check whether residents declare their foreign accounts.", "thread_id": "2026-10-15-news-swiss-share-data-on-4-million-accounts", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-15", "event_date": "2026-10-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Tax Administration", "AEOI", "OECD"]}
